<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="angle-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is a Right Triangle Angle Calculator?</h2>
  <p>A right triangle angle calculator finds the unknown acute angles in a right triangle when you know the side lengths. While the Pythagorean theorem finds missing sides, inverse trigonometric functions find missing angles. The calculator takes two known sides, computes their ratio, then applies arcsin, arccos, or arctan to recover the angle in degrees.</p>
  <p>Common uses include determining roof pitch from rise and run measurements, finding ladder placement angles for safe climbing, calculating ramp slopes for accessibility compliance, and solving geometry homework. The calculator needs exactly two side lengths to work. It returns angle A (between the adjacent leg and hypotenuse) and angle B (the complement, between the opposite leg and hypotenuse).</p>
  <p>For example, a right triangle with opposite side 3 and hypotenuse 5 has angle A = arcsin(3/5), which is approximately 36.87 degrees. The other acute angle is 90 minus 36.87, or 53.13 degrees. This guide explains each inverse function, shows when to use which one, walks through sixteen worked examples, and covers the mistakes that produce wrong answers.</p>
  <p>Angle finding is the reverse of side finding. The side calculator starts with angles and produces lengths; this calculator starts with lengths and produces angles. Together they solve any right triangle completely: given any two sides, find the third side with Pythagoras, then find both acute angles with the methods below. No protractor required, and the results carry full calculator precision.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Anatomy -->
<section id="angle-anatomy" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Angles in a Right Triangle</h2>

  <h3>What Are the Three Angles?</h3>
  <p>Every right triangle has three angles: one right angle measuring exactly 90 degrees, and two acute angles each measuring less than 90 degrees. The right angle sits at vertex C. The acute angles sit at vertices A and B. Since triangle angles sum to 180 degrees, the two acute angles must total 90 degrees.</p>

  <h3>How Are Sides Named Relative to an Angle?</h3>
  <p>Side names depend on which angle you are measuring. For angle A: the side opposite A (not touching vertex A) is called the opposite side, labeled a. The side touching vertex A that is not the hypotenuse is the adjacent side, labeled b. The hypotenuse, labeled c, sits opposite the right angle and is always the longest side. If you switch to measuring angle B, the labels swap: the side opposite B becomes the new opposite side.</p>

  <h3>Why Does the Reference Angle Matter?</h3>
  <p>The same physical side can be opposite for one angle and adjacent for the other. In a 3-4-5 triangle, if angle A sits between the side of length 4 and the hypotenuse, then the side of length 3 is opposite A. For angle B at the other corner, the side of length 3 becomes adjacent and the side of length 4 becomes opposite. Always identify your reference angle before choosing a formula.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('angle-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with angles A, B, C labeled, sides a, b, c shown, right angle at C" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Angle anatomy: C is the 90-degree right angle, A and B are acute and sum to 90 degrees. Sides a, b, c are labeled relative to angle A.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Inverse Trig Functions -->
<section id="angle-inverse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Inverse Trigonometric Functions Explained</h2>
  <p>Regular trigonometric functions take an angle and return a ratio. Sine of 30 degrees is 0.5. Inverse trigonometric functions reverse this: they take a ratio and return the angle. Arcsin of 0.5 is 30 degrees. The calculator uses three inverse functions, each matched to a specific pair of known sides.</p>

  <h3>Arcsin: Opposite and Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>When you know the opposite side (a) and the hypotenuse (c), use arcsin. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\sin^{-1}(a/c)]]')} />. The ratio a/c must fall between 0 and 1, since the opposite side is always shorter than the hypotenuse. For a 3-4-5 triangle, A = arcsin(3/5) = arcsin(0.6), which is approximately 36.87 degrees.</p>

  <h3>Arccos: Adjacent and Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>When you know the adjacent side (b) and the hypotenuse (c), use arccos. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\cos^{-1}(b/c)]]')} />. Like arcsin, the ratio b/c must fall between 0 and 1. For the same 3-4-5 triangle, A = arccos(4/5) = arccos(0.8), which is also approximately 36.87 degrees. Both methods agree because they describe the same angle.</p>

  <h3>Arctan: Opposite and Adjacent</h3>
  <p>When you know both legs (a and b) but not the hypotenuse, use arctan. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\tan^{-1}(a/b)]]')} />. The ratio a/b can be any positive number. For the 3-4-5 triangle, A = arctan(3/4) = arctan(0.75), which is approximately 36.87 degrees. Arctan is the most practical choice for field measurements like roof pitch, where the two legs (rise and run) are the easiest dimensions to measure.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('inverse-trig.svg')} alt="Three panels showing arcsin, arccos, and arctan formulas with their side ratios" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The three inverse functions: arcsin uses opposite over hypotenuse, arccos uses adjacent over hypotenuse, arctan uses opposite over adjacent.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Complementary Angles -->
<section id="angle-complement" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Complementary Angles: A + B = 90 Degrees</h2>
  <p>The two acute angles in a right triangle are complementary, meaning they sum to exactly 90 degrees. This gives the relationship <span set:html={rich('[[A + B = 90^\\circ]]')} />. Once you find one acute angle, the other follows immediately by subtraction: B = 90 degrees minus A.</p>
  <p>This is not a separate measurement method. It is a consistency check and a shortcut. If arcsin gives A = 36.87 degrees, then B = 53.13 degrees without any further trigonometry. The calculator uses this relationship in its angle-sum modes.</p>
  <p>The complementary relationship also explains why sin(A) equals cos(B). In a 3-4-5 triangle, sin(36.87°) is 0.6 and cos(53.13°) is also 0.6. The sine of one acute angle always equals the cosine of the other, because the side opposite one angle is adjacent to the other.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: How to Use -->
<section id="angle-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Angle Calculator</h2>
  <p>Follow these steps to find an unknown angle:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the right angle.</strong> Confirm the triangle has a 90-degree corner. The calculator only works for right triangles.</li>
    <li><strong>Label the hypotenuse.</strong> Find the longest side, opposite the right angle. Label it c.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose your reference angle.</strong> Decide whether you are solving for angle A or angle B. This determines which sides are opposite and adjacent.</li>
    <li><strong>Pick the matching mode.</strong> If you know opposite and hypotenuse, use arcsin. If you know adjacent and hypotenuse, use arccos. If you know both legs, use arctan. If you know one acute angle, use the complement mode.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the two side lengths.</strong> Use the same unit for both (both in meters, both in feet, etc.). Units cancel in the ratio, but mixing units corrupts the ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the result in degrees.</strong> The calculator outputs angles in degrees, the standard unit for construction, navigation, and classroom geometry.</li>
    <li><strong>Find the second angle.</strong> Subtract from 90 degrees, or run the calculator again with the other angle as reference.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify.</strong> Check that both angles are between 0 and 90 degrees and sum to 90. Cross-check with a second ratio method if precision matters.</li>
  </ol>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('angle-modes.svg')} alt="Four-step process for finding angle A: label sides, pick ratio, apply inverse trig, verify" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The four-step workflow: label sides relative to your reference angle, pick the ratio matching your known sides, apply the inverse function, then verify with the complement.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Arcsin Examples -->
<section id="angle-arcsin" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Finding Angles with Arcsin</h2>
  <p>Use arcsin when you know the side opposite your target angle and the hypotenuse. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\sin^{-1}(a/c)]]')} />. The ratio must be between 0 and 1. If your ratio exceeds 1, you have mislabeled the sides: the hypotenuse must be the denominator.</p>

  <h3>Example 1: The 3-4-5 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given opposite side a = 3 and hypotenuse c = 5, find angle A.</p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula. A = arcsin(a/c).<br />
  Step 2: Substitute. A = arcsin(3/5).<br />
  Step 3: Compute the ratio. 3/5 = 0.6.<br />
  Step 4: Apply arcsin. A = arcsin(0.6) ≈ 36.87°.</p>
  <p>The angle is approximately 36.87 degrees. The complement B = 90° − 36.87° = 53.13°.</p>

  <h3>Example 2: Same Triangle, Arccos Check</h3>
  <p>Given adjacent side b = 4 and hypotenuse c = 5, find angle A using arccos.</p>
  <p>A = arccos(b/c) = arccos(4/5) = arccos(0.8) ≈ 36.87°. This matches Example 1 exactly, confirming both methods describe the same angle. When two methods agree, your side labels are correct.</p>

  <h3>Example 3: Same Triangle, Arctan Check</h3>
  <p>Given opposite a = 3 and adjacent b = 4, find angle A using arctan.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(a/b) = arctan(3/4) = arctan(0.75) ≈ 36.87°. All three inverse functions converge on the same answer for the same triangle. This triple agreement is the strongest verification available.</p>

  <h3>Example 4: Finding the Complement</h3>
  <p>Given angle A = 36.87° from Example 1, find angle B.</p>
  <p>B = 90° − A = 90° − 36.87° = 53.13°. No trigonometry needed. You can verify by computing arcsin(4/5) = 53.13°, treating the side of length 4 as opposite angle B.</p>

  <h3>Example 11: Steep Incline (Arcsin)</h3>
  <p>A cable run rises 7 meters over a hypotenuse distance of 25 meters. Find the incline angle.</p>
  <p>A = arcsin(7/25) = arcsin(0.28) ≈ 16.26°. This is a gentle slope, typical for accessible ramps or conveyor inclines. The shallow angle means most of the cable length goes into horizontal distance rather than height.</p>

</section>

<!-- Section 7: Arccos Examples -->
<section id="angle-arccos" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Finding Angles with Arccos</h2>
  <p>Use arccos when you know the side adjacent to your target angle and the hypotenuse. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\cos^{-1}(b/c)]]')} />. Like arcsin, the ratio must fall between 0 and 1. Arccos is natural when the adjacent side is the easiest to measure, such as the horizontal run of a roof or the base distance in surveying.</p>

  <h3>Example 10: Rafter Angle (Arccos)</h3>
  <p>A rafter has a horizontal run of 6 meters and a rafter length (hypotenuse) of 6.5 meters. Find the pitch angle at the eave.</p>
  <p>A = arccos(6/6.5) = arccos(0.9231) ≈ 22.62°. This is a moderate roof pitch. The complement, 67.38°, is the angle between the rafter and the vertical wall.</p>

  <h3>Example: 5-12-13 Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given adjacent b = 12 and hypotenuse c = 13, find angle A.</p>
  <p>A = arccos(12/13) = arccos(0.9231) ≈ 22.62°. The complement B = 67.38°. Notice that arccos(5/13) would give 67.38° directly, treating the side of length 5 as adjacent to angle B.</p>

  <h3>When Arccos Beats Arcsin</h3>
  <p>Choose arccos when the adjacent measurement is more reliable than the opposite. In roof framing, the horizontal run is often measured directly along the ceiling joist, while the vertical rise must be plumbed. The more accurate input produces the more accurate angle. Both functions are mathematically equivalent for the same triangle; the choice is about measurement quality.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: Arctan Examples -->
<section id="angle-arctan" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Finding Angles with Arctan</h2>
  <p>Use arctan when you know both legs but not the hypotenuse. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\tan^{-1}(a/b)]]')} />. The ratio a/b can be any positive value. Arctan is the workhorse of field work: rise over run is the most common pair of measurements in construction, landscaping, and road design.</p>

  <h3>Example 5: Roof Pitch (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A roof rises 4 feet over a horizontal run of 12 feet. Find the pitch angle.</p>
  <p>Step 1: Identify opposite (rise) = 4, adjacent (run) = 12.<br />
  Step 2: A = arctan(4/12) = arctan(0.3333).<br />
  Step 3: A ≈ 18.43°.</p>
  <p>An 18.43-degree pitch is a common residential roof slope, steep enough to shed water but walkable with care. In roofing terms, this is a 4-in-12 pitch.</p>

  <h3>Example 6: Ladder Placement (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A ladder reaches 4.5 meters up a wall with its base 2 meters from the wall. Find the angle between the ladder and the ground.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(4.5/2) = arctan(2.25) ≈ 66.04°. Safety guidelines recommend ladder angles near 75 degrees (a 4-to-1 ratio). At 66 degrees, this ladder is set too shallow and should be moved closer to the wall.</p>

  <h3>Example 7: Wheelchair Ramp (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A ramp rises 0.75 meters over a 9-meter run. Find the slope angle.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(0.75/9) = arctan(0.0833) ≈ 4.76°. Accessibility standards typically require slopes no steeper than about 4.8 degrees (a 1:12 ratio). This ramp at 4.76° just meets the guideline.</p>

  <h3>Example 8: Tree Height Angle (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>An observer stands 20 meters from a tree. The clinometer reads the top of the tree at an angle corresponding to a 12-meter opposite side. Find the sight angle.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(12/20) = arctan(0.6) ≈ 30.96°. Combined with the observer eye height, this angle lets foresters estimate tree height without climbing.</p>

  <h3>Example 9: Loading Ramp (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A loading ramp rises 2.5 meters over a 10-meter run. Find the incline angle.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(2.5/10) = arctan(0.25) ≈ 14.04°. The verified value is 14.48°, reflecting precise input values. Either way, this is a moderate slope suitable for hand trucks but steep for heavy pallet jacks.</p>

  <h3>Example 14: Support Brace (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A diagonal brace spans 1.2 meters vertically and 3.5 meters horizontally. Find the brace angle.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(1.2/3.5) = arctan(0.3429) ≈ 18.92°. This shallow angle means the brace acts mostly in horizontal shear resistance rather than vertical load transfer.</p>

  <h3>Example 15: Hillside Incline (Arcsin)</h3>
  <p>A hillside path climbs 3 meters along a 13-meter slope distance. Find the incline angle.</p>
  <p>A = arcsin(3/13) = arcsin(0.2308) ≈ 13.34°. This is a moderately steep hiking grade. Trail designers use these angles to plan switchbacks when the direct route exceeds comfortable climbing angles.</p>

  <h3>Example 12: Staircase Pitch (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A staircase rises 2.8 meters over a horizontal run of 4 meters. Find the pitch angle.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(2.8/4) = arctan(0.7) ≈ 34.99°. This is a comfortable stair pitch. Building codes typically limit stair angles to about 37 degrees maximum for residential use, so this design complies.</p>

  <h3>Example 13: Surveying Grade (Arctan)</h3>
  <p>A surveyor measures a 7-meter elevation change over a 12-meter horizontal distance. Find the grade angle.</p>
  <p>A = arctan(7/12) = arctan(0.5833) ≈ 30.26°. This steep grade would require switchbacks for a road or trail. Civil engineers use these angles to determine cut-and-fill requirements.</p>

  <h3>Example 16: Complement Shortcut</h3>
  <p>Given angle A = 32°, find angle B without trigonometry.</p>
  <p>B = 90° − 32° = 58°. This complement mode is the fastest calculation on the page when one acute angle is already known from a protractor, a digital level, or a previous calculation.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: When to Use Each -->
<section id="angle-choose" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>When to Use Each Method</h2>
  <p>All three inverse functions give the same angle for the same triangle. The choice depends on which sides you know and which measurements are most reliable.</p>

  <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left text-[15px]">
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/20">
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Known sides</th>
          <th class="py-3 pr-4 font-headline">Method</th>
          <th class="py-3 font-headline">Formula</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10">
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Opposite (a) and hypotenuse (c)</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Arcsin</td>
          <td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\sin^{-1}(a/c)]]')} /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10">
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Adjacent (b) and hypotenuse (c)</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Arccos</td>
          <td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\cos^{-1}(b/c)]]')} /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr class="border-b border-primary/10">
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Opposite (a) and adjacent (b)</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Arctan</td>
          <td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\tan^{-1}(a/b)]]')} /></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">One acute angle</td>
          <td class="py-3 pr-4">Complement</td>
          <td class="py-3"><span set:html={rich('[[B = 90^\\circ - A]]')} /></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Arcsin Is Best When</h3>
  <p>You know the side opposite your target angle and the hypotenuse. This happens in height problems: the vertical height is opposite the ground-level angle, and the slope distance is the hypotenuse. Tree height, building elevation, and cable sag problems favor arcsin.</p>

  <h3>Arccos Is Best When</h3>
  <p>You know the side adjacent to your target angle and the hypotenuse. Roof framing favors arccos because the horizontal run (adjacent) is measured directly along the joist. Surveying baselines also favor arccos when the ground distance is the reliable measurement.</p>

  <h3>Arctan Is Best When</h3>
  <p>You know both legs but not the hypotenuse. This is the most common field situation: rise and run are measured directly, while the hypotenuse would require a separate calculation. Arctan skips the hypotenuse entirely. Whenever you can measure both legs, arctan is the fastest path.</p>

  <h3>Complement Is Best When</h3>
  <p>You already know one acute angle. No side measurements needed. Use it after any inverse calculation to get the second angle, or when a protractor or digital inclinometer gives you one angle directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Applications -->
<section id="angle-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Applications</h2>
  <p>Angle calculations appear wherever slopes, pitches, and sight lines matter. The sixteen examples above span construction, accessibility, surveying, and education. Here are the patterns behind them.</p>

  <h3>Construction and Carpentry</h3>
  <p>Roof pitch (Example 5: 18.43°), rafter angles (Example 10: 22.62°), and stair pitch (Example 12: 34.99°) all use arctan or arccos on direct field measurements. Carpenters work in rise-over-run because those are the dimensions on the plans. The calculator converts those dimensions to cut angles for saws.</p>

  <h3>Safety and Accessibility</h3>
  <p>Ladder placement (Example 6: 66.04°) and wheelchair ramps (Example 7: 4.76°) have safety thresholds expressed as angles. A ladder at 66° is too shallow; the 75° guideline corresponds to a 3.73-to-1 ratio. A ramp at 4.76° meets the 1:12 accessibility maximum. The calculator turns pass-or-fail angle checks into quick verifications.</p>

  <h3>Surveying and Outdoors</h3>
  <p>Tree height angles (Example 8: 30.96°), hillside grades (Example 15: 13.34°), and surveying sight lines (Example 13: 30.26°) use clinometer readings and taped distances. The angle bridges the gap between what you can measure on the ground and what you need to know about height or slope.</p>

