<!-- Section 11: Mistakes -->
<section id="angle-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Finding Angles</h2>

  <h3>Swapping Opposite and Adjacent</h3>
  <p>The most frequent error. The labels depend on your reference angle. If you compute arctan(4/3) = 53.13° when you meant arctan(3/4) = 36.87°, you have solved for the wrong angle. Fix: sketch the triangle, mark your reference angle, then label opposite (across from it) and adjacent (touching it).</p>

  <h3>Using the Wrong Inverse Function</h3>
  <p>Applying arcsin to an adjacent-over-hypotenuse ratio gives the complement of the intended angle, not an error message. Arcsin(4/5) = 53.13° is angle B, not angle A. Fix: match the function to your known sides using the table above.</p>

  <h3>Forgetting the Ratio Must Be Valid</h3>
  <p>Arcsin and arccos require ratios between 0 and 1. If you enter opposite = 5 and hypotenuse = 3, the ratio 1.67 has no arcsin. This always means mislabeled sides: the hypotenuse must be the longest side and the denominator. Fix: reassign the longest measurement as the hypotenuse.</p>

  <h3>Mixing Degrees and Radians</h3>
  <p>Inverse trig on calculators and in code often defaults to radians. Arctan(1) = 45° in degrees but 0.785 in radians. This calculator outputs degrees. If your hand calculation gives 0.64 for a 3-4-5 triangle, you are in radians; multiply by 180/π to get 36.87°.</p>

  <h3>Entering Sides in Different Units</h3>
  <p>Opposite in inches and hypotenuse in feet corrupts the ratio. Convert to a common unit first. The ratio is unitless, but only when both inputs share the unit.</p>

  <h3>Rounding the Ratio Too Early</h3>
  <p>Arcsin(0.6) = 36.87°, but arcsin(0.60) rounded from 0.596 gives 36.58°. Small ratio errors magnify near 0 and 1 where the inverse functions are steepest. Keep full precision through the calculation, round only the final angle.</p>

  <h3>Assuming the Calculator Finds Sides</h3>
  <p>This page finds angles from sides. It does not find sides from angles. For side-from-angle problems, use the dedicated side calculator. Each tool has one job.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12b: Precision -->
<section id="angle-precision" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Angle Precision and Units</h2>
  <p>Angle results are only as precise as the side measurements behind them. A tape measure reading to the nearest millimeter supports two decimal places in the angle. A paced-off distance in the field supports whole degrees at best. The calculator computes with full floating-point precision; the rounding decision is yours.</p>

  <h3>How Input Error Affects the Angle</h3>
  <p>Small side errors cause larger angle errors when the ratio sits near 0 or 1, where inverse functions are steepest. For arctan near 45°, a 1% error in the ratio shifts the angle by about 0.3°. Near 5° or 85°, the same 1% ratio error shifts the angle by over 0.5°. Measure carefully at extreme angles, or take multiple readings and average.</p>

  <h3>Degrees vs. Degrees-Minutes-Seconds</h3>
  <p>Surveyors sometimes express angles as degrees, minutes, and seconds (DMS). To convert 36.87° to DMS: the whole degrees are 36, the fractional 0.87° times 60 gives 52.2 minutes, and 0.2 minutes times 60 gives 12 seconds, yielding 36° 52′ 12″. This calculator outputs decimal degrees; multiply the fraction by 60 for minutes.</p>

  <h3>When Whole Degrees Suffice</h3>
  <p>Carpentry cut angles, ladder placement checks, and rough grade estimates rarely need decimals. Roofing pitch to the nearest degree is plenty for material ordering. Reserve two-decimal precision for machining, optical alignment, and engineering calculations where tolerances are tight.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Verify -->
<section id="angle-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Verifying Your Angle Answer</h2>
  <p>Three checks confirm a correct angle calculation:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Range check.</strong> Both acute angles must fall strictly between 0° and 90°. An angle of 0°, 90°, or above signals invalid inputs or a mislabeled triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Sum check.</strong> A + B must equal exactly 90°. If your two angles sum to 89.9° or 90.1°, rounding explains it. Larger gaps indicate an error.</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-method check.</strong> Recompute the same angle with a different inverse function. If arcsin(3/5) = 36.87° and arctan(3/4) = 36.87°, the answer is solid. Disagreement points to a labeling error.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For critical work, also verify the sides satisfy the Pythagorean theorem. In a 3-4-5 triangle, 9 + 16 = 25 confirms the sides before you trust the angles derived from them.</p>
</section>


